<h2>Translating the Perforated Façade to 1:200 Scale</h2>
<p>The building’s perforated façade contained openings that were too fine to reproduce literally at 1:200 scale. If reduced directly, many of the openings would become visually unclear or impractical to fabricate consistently.</p>
<p>Carve adjusted the opening size, spacing, and density to preserve the façade’s overall visual character at model scale. The resulting pattern communicates the intended transparency, rhythm, and surface texture while remaining durable and legible as a physical model.</p>

<h2>Coordinating Equipment, Color and Lighting</h2>
<p>The exposed laboratory required representative equipment to be coordinated with the architectural layout. Available drawings, elevations, reference images, and color information were reviewed to establish the placement, proportions, and appearance of the principal interior components.</p>
<p>Integrated lighting was used to make the cutaway laboratory easier to read and to distinguish the interior program from the surrounding building. Equipment colors, lighting, façade finishes, and other visible components were reviewed together so the completed model would present the campus as one coordinated physical composition.</p>

<h2>Coordinated Delivery and Setup</h2>
<p>The completed model required protective packaging, coordinated transportation, and careful handling at the final display location. Carve planned the delivery and setup around the model’s dimensions, illuminated components, exposed interior details, and other fragile elements to reduce unnecessary handling and protect the finished work.</p>

<h2>Project Outcome</h2>
<p>The completed 1:200 model brought the campus architecture, technical laboratory environment, façade design, landscape, and lighting into one physical presentation. The cutaway configuration allowed viewers to understand both the building’s exterior character and the organization of the laboratory interior at the same time.</p>
